## Additional Optimizations

### Use tmpfs / RAM disk to eliminate disk I/O

```ksh
# One-time setup (needs root)
mount -t tmpfs -o size=64G tmpfs /mnt/ramfs

# Verify
df -h /mnt/ramfs
```

### Parallel worker tuning for 12 vCPU

```
zcat    → 1 CPU core per worker (decompression)
openssl → 1 CPU core per worker (AES-NI hardware accelerated)
s3 cp   → mostly network/IO wait, low CPU

Optimal workers = 12 (CPU-bound on zcat+openssl)
Each pipeline: ~80-100 MB/s
Total: 12 × ~90 MB/s = ~1 GB/s (network permitting)
```

### Check AES-NI hardware acceleration

```ksh
# Confirm AES-NI is available (critical for encryption speed)
grep -m1 aes /proc/cpuinfo

# Benchmark openssl throughput
openssl speed -evp aes-256-ctr

# Should show ~3-5 GB/s with AES-NI
# If slow → switch to chacha20 (software-friendly)
openssl enc -chacha20 ...
```

### AWS CLI multipart tuning for streaming

```ini
[default]
s3 =
    max_concurrent_requests = 64
    multipart_threshold     = 64MB
    multipart_chunksize     = 64MB    # larger chunks = fewer requests
    max_queue_size          = 1000
```

